      "summary_raw": "Bloom Energy Estimates $3.6 Billion in Equipment Cost Savings from 800V DC Conversion for AI Data Centers finance.biggo.com",
      "source_text": "Bloom Energy Estimates $3.6 Billion in Equipment Cost Savings from 800V DC Conversion for AI Data Centers Add preferred source Bloom Energy, a leading U.S. fuel cell manufacturer, released an economic analysis on September 16 for an 800V DC-native power architecture designed for 1GW-class AI data centers. The company estimates that compared with conventional AC power delivery, the approach can reduce non-compute capital expenditures by $3.6 billion (27%) and five-year total cost of ownership by $5.5 billion (9%). Bloom Energy proposes outputting 800V DC directly from its solid oxide fuel cells. An industry survey also projected that DC-based configurations will account for 58% of deployments by 2030. Separately, the company is set to join the S&P 500 on September 21, with its stock trading near record highs.\nBloom Energy, a leading U.S. fuel cell manufacturer, released an economic analysis on September 16 for an 800V DC-native power architecture designed for 1-gigawatt (GW)-class AI data centers. According to the company's estimates, compared with conventional alternating current (AC) power intake and distribution, the approach can reduce non-compute capital expenditures—excluding servers, GPUs, and other computing equipment—by $3.6 billion (approximately ¥560 billion, or 27%), and is projected to deliver $5.5 billion (approximately ¥860 billion, or 9%) in savings over a five-year total cost of ownership (TCO) period. The announcement signals that the drive to rethink power delivery architecture inside data centers is gaining real momentum, fueled by surging AI-driven electricity demand. Beyond the supply-side challenges of generation capacity and grid interconnection, the company is focusing on how delivered power actually reaches the GPUs—the intake and distribution architecture itself. How 800V DC-Native Delivery Works In today's typical data center, power is received from the grid as AC and converted through multiple stages—transformers, switchgear, and uninterruptible power supplies (UPS)—before ultimately reaching the DC power that computing equipment requires. Bloom Energy's proposal instead outputs 800V DC directly from its solid oxide fuel cells (SOFCs), reducing reliance on intermediate AC/DC conversion steps and transformers in a native delivery configuration. The company argues that eliminating power conversion equipment lowers upfront capital investment while also reducing conversion losses. The estimated $3.6 billion reduction in non-compute CAPEX for a 1GW-scale model quantifies the economic advantage of this approach. It is important to note, however, that these figures are model-based estimates derived from assumptions set by the company, not verified operational results from large-scale data center deployments. The savings also apply only to the non-compute portion of data center construction investment, not the total build cost. It is equally critical not to conflate the 27% equipment cost reduction with a reduction in power consumption. Industry Survey Shows DC-Based Configurations Becoming the Majority In a survey of 156 data center industry decision-makers conducted by Bloom Energy in 2026, respondents indicated that by 2030, DC-based configurations—combining hybrid AC-DC and DC-native architectures—would account for 58% of data center deployments. This is presented as a forward-looking projection based on an industry survey, not a confirmed share forecast for the global market as a whole. Driven by the increasing density of AI servers, the industry has been exploring a shift toward DC power architectures, including 800V DC. The savings figures Bloom Energy has presented are model estimates, and actual results will vary depending on site location, energy procurement costs, and equipment pricing. Going forward, the key question is how widely 800V DC-native delivery will be adopted in actual large-scale projects and whether the modeled economics hold up in real-world operations. Stock Trading Near Highs Ahead of S&P 500 Inclusion Separately, the company is scheduled to join the S&P 500 index before the start of trading on September 21, which will trigger buying demand from index-tracking funds. S&P Dow Jones Indices formally announced the change earlier this month. Bloom Energy's stock has roughly tripled in 2026 and recently traded around $263 per share—about 25% below its 52-week high of $351.28. Following the index inclusion announcement, the stock rose approximately 10% in the first trading session but subsequently gave back much of those gains, settling about 4% above its pre-announcement close. Research from Harvard Business School academics shows that the additional return associated with S&P 500 inclusion has declined from an average of 7.4% in the 1990s to just 0.3% in the decade through 2020, reflecting the market's growing tendency to price in index fund buying in advance. Rapidly Expanding Results, Full-Year Guidance Raised Twice The company's financial performance is expandi",
