      "summary_raw": "Will 800V AI Data Center Wins Change Bloom Energy Stock Narrative simplywall.st",
      "source_text": "Advertisement United States / Electrical / NYSE:BE Will 800V AI Data Center Wins Change Bloom Energy Stock Narrative September 17, 2026 SI Simply Wall St Reviewed by Sasha Jovanovic Bloom Energy released detailed analysis of its 800V DC-native fuel cell systems for AI data centers, highlighting potential capital and operating cost savings, as well as faster on-site power deployment compared with traditional AC-based infrastructure. The business is pairing this 800V architecture push with growing AI data center deployments and confirmed inclusion in the S&P 500 in September 2026. This signals a shift from early adoption to wider commercial use across large power users. The next area of focus is how Bloom Energy's 800V AI data center strategy could influence the longer term investment narrative for the company. Scan beyond Bloom Energy and see which other AI infrastructure players are already on investors' radar with the hand picked 60 AI infrastructure stocks . Advertisement What Is Bloom Energy's Investment Narrative? To own Bloom Energy, you have to believe the fuel cell platform can keep turning AI data center demand into durable cash generation despite heavy capital needs. The pitch is simple on paper. An 800V DC-native system that cuts non-compute capex and power losses for hyperscalers, backed by real deployments like Project Phoenix and partnerships with Oracle and Brookfield. The new report on cost savings and faster deployment feeds directly into that story, because it targets the exact pain point that is driving AI infrastructure spending right now. In the short term, the inclusion in the S&P 500, recent share price performance, and optimistic sell-side research mainly function as supporting context around that operational thesis. The more important near-term catalysts are continued order flow from AI data centers, execution on nearly 1.4 GW already deployed, and the ability to protect pricing while funding a capital-intensive rollout. High share price volatility, shareholder dilution in the past year, one-off items in earnings and reliance on higher-risk funding remain key factors that could challenge this story if growth weakens. Even so, under all the AI excitement and index headlines, there is a less comfortable pressure point forming around ... There's only one way to know the right time to buy, sell or hold Bloom Energy. Head to Simply Wall St's company report for the latest analysis of Bloom Energy's Fair Value . NYSE:BE 1-Year Stock Price Chart Exploring Other Perspectives For Bloom Energy, the optimistic swing factor is earnings power. The most bullish analysts were modeling revenue climbing fast enough to reach about US$17.8b with earnings of US$5.9b by 2029, compared with US$244.9m today. Those forecasts came before this 800V report, so you should expect opinions to shift as new data lands. Explore 5 other Bloom Energy fair value estimates , including one that suggests there could be as much as 44% upside from the current price. Form Your Own Verdict Disagree with this assessment? Extraordinary investment returns rarely come from following the herd, so go with your instincts. A great starting point for your Bloom Energy research is our analysis highlighting 3 key rewards and 3 important warning signs that could impact your investment decision. See our latest analysis for Bloom Energy. The report includes a comprehensive fundamental analysis summarized in a single visual, the Snowflake, making it easy to evaluate Bloom Energy's overall financial health at a glance. Looking For More Investment Ideas Beyond Bloom Energy? Once you have a view on Bloom Energy, it can help to widen the lens and compare its setup with other listed businesses using the Simply Wall St Screener. This gives you a broader sense of what risk, quality and income profiles are available across the market right now. If you want potential value with quality baked in, scan through a 33 high quality undervalued stocks tha