      "summary_raw": "IAA TRANSPORTATION: Inmotion Technologies expands power electronics range ivtinternational.com",
      "source_text": "Featured 29th July 2026 iVT July/August issue – Digital edition available here! iVT By Anjali Sooknanan\nRecent iVT July/August issue – Digital edition available here! 29th July 2026\niVT May/June issue – Digital edition available here! 18th May 2026\niVT February/March issue – Digital edition available here! 13th February 2026\nInmotion Technologies, a Zapi Group company, is presenting its latest motive power products at IAA Transportation in Hannover, running from 15–20 September. The company is showing three applications: a third-generation high-voltage inverter, a new DC/DC converter for auxiliary systems, and a preview of an integrated motor inverter for electric power take-off (PTO) applications. The ACH3 inverter powers a vehicle’s motor with what the company states is 99% peak efficiency and best-in-class EMC performance. Current ratings for the ACH3 family run from 30 to more than 600 amperes and up to 900V bus voltage at full power. All units are assembled in Sweden and feature built-in native cybersecurity and functional safety. The DCC3 is a compact DC/DC converter engineered for a range of commercial and construction vehicle applications. It converts input voltages from 250 to 900V into a stable, adjustable 12 or 24V output, delivering up to 10kW of power for auxiliary systems in electric or hybrid industrial, commercial and utility vehicles. The IMI 214 is a European-engineered integrated motor inverter developed with Schabmüller, also a ZAPI Group company. It combines power electronics and electric motor technology in a compact package for electric PTO applications in commercial vehicles. The company says the design is optimised for performance, efficiency and ease of integration into existing vehicle architectures. “We are excited to showcase these new capabilities at IAA Transportation 2026 because they address real integration challenges facing commercial vehicle OEMs,” says Martin Wennerblom, product and marketing director at Inmotion Technologies. “By combining safety, performance, and streamlined installation, our solutions help manufacturers reduce integration effort while supporting the transition toward more sustainable and efficient commercial vehicles.” Inmotion is exhibiting at Hall 12, Stand D40, alongside Business Sweden and FKG. Image: Inmotion Technologies\nPrevious Article TTControl expands physical AI and autonomy offering\nAnjali Sooknanan Anjali Sooknanan is deputy editor of the iVT brand - which includes digital and print editions of a quarterly magazine and Off-Highway Annual, as well as ivtinternational.com. She holds an English degree from Goldsmiths University in London, and has experience working on B2B publications in various industries from electrical to construction.\nRelated Posts Autonomous Vehicles TTControl expands physical AI and autonomy offering 16th September 2026 3 Mins Read\nAwards & Events IAA TRANSPORTATION: Stoneridge unveils EVO ECU platform for off-highway use 14th September 2026 3 Mins Read\nElectronics HMI Systems launches rugged 750MDI touch display 10th September 2026 2 Mins Read\nLatest Posts IAA TRANSPORTATION: Inmotion Technologies expands power electronics range 17th September 2026\nTTControl expands physical AI and autonomy offering 16th September 2026\nVolvo CE launches redesigned L30 and L35 wheel loaders 16th September 2026\nInmotion Technologies, a Zapi Group company, is presenting its latest motive power products at IAA Transportation in Hannover, running from 15–20 September.\nThe company is showing three applications: a third-generation high-voltage inverter, a new DC/DC converter for auxiliary systems, and a preview of an integrated motor inverter for electric power take-off (PTO) applications.\nThe ACH3 inverter powers a vehicle’s motor with what the company states is 99% peak efficiency and best-in-class EMC performance. Current ratings for the ACH3 family run from 30 to more than 600 amperes and up to 900V bus voltage at full power. All units are assembled in Sweden and feature built-in native cybersecurity and functional safety.\nThe DCC3 is a compact DC/DC converter engineered for a range of commercial and construction vehicle applications. It converts input voltages from 250 to 900V into a stable, adjustable 12 or 24V output, delivering up to 10kW of power for auxiliary systems in electric or hybrid industrial, commercial and utility vehicles.\nThe IMI 214 is a European-engineered integrated motor inverter developed with Schabmüller, also a ZAPI Group company. It combines power electronics and electric motor technology in a compact package for electric PTO applications in commercial vehicles. The company says the design is optimised for performance, efficiency and ease of integration into existing vehicle architectures.\n“We are excited to showcase these new capabilities at IAA Transportation 2026 because they address real integration challenges facing commercial vehicle OEMs,” says Martin Wennerblom, product and marketing director at Inmotion ",
