      "source_text": "Home › News › Rohm SiC chips power BMW Neue Klasse EV architecture Rohm SiC chips power BMW Neue Klasse EV architecture Rohm's confirmation as a SiC supplier to Neue Klasse signals the competitive pressure to secure design wins in premium EV platforms By Automotive World September 17, 2026 Rohm Semiconductor has confirmed that its silicon carbide (SiC) chips are integrated into the powertrain of BMW’s Neue Klasse generation of electric vehicles, supporting energy efficiency, driving range and charging performance. The components mark Rohm’s presence in BMW Group’s Gen 6 electric vehicle (EV) architecture. Rohm’s SiC components support key power electronics functions within the Neue Klasse powertrain, with the chips designed to reduce energy losses under demanding operating conditions. Their integration is intended to contribute to improvements in driving range, charging performance and overall energy efficiency. Rohm has extensive experience manufacturing power semiconductors for automotive applications and works with vehicle manufacturers and Tier 1 suppliers globally. The BMW Group collaboration represents the firm’s contribution to what it describes as the electrification and decarbonisation of mobility. Wolfram Harnack, President of Rohm Semiconductor GmbH, said: “The integration of our SiC MOSFETs into BMW’s Gen 6 of electric vehicles—better known as Neue Klasse—highlights Rohm’s technological expertise in automotive power electronics. We are proud to contribute to the advancement of next-generation electric mobility together with BMW Group through our SiC technology. It underlines our long-term commitment to supporting our automotive customers with high-quality and highly reliable products.” Source: Rohm Semiconductor\nSeptember 17, 2026 Will VW say ‘adios’ to SEAT before 2030?\nSeptember 17, 2026 BYD needs three Europe car plants and a battery factory\nSeptember 17, 2026 FT: EU asks China to cap hybrid exports, threatens tariffs\nSearch 