      "source_text": "Why Could Bloom Energy (BE) Change AI Data Center Power Economics? Bailey Pemberton Thu, 17 September 2026 at 6:13 am GMT-5 2 min read BE\nCopyright © 2026 Yahoo. All rights reserved. What's trending Straits Times Index Nikkei Hang Seng FTSE 100 Bitcoin USD TSLA\nTerms and Privacy Policy Privacy dashboard Ad terms Feedback\nSkip to navigation Skip to main content Skip to right column Yahoo Finance News Finance More News Today's news Singapore Politics Southeast Asia World Sports Personal Finance Property Weather Lifestyle Travel Health Fashion Celebrity Parenting Food Finance My portfolio Markets Watchlists Screeners Money News Cryptocurrencies Industries Property Sign in My Portfolio News Stocks Singapore Economics Property Tech Crypto Currencies Commodities Tax Budget 2024 Videos Markets Cryptocurrencies Calendar Volume Leaders Currencies World Indices Price Gainers Price Losers Commodities Stock Comparison Currency Converter Screeners ... Singapore markets open in 8h 13m Singapore Singapore US Markets Europe Markets Asia Markets Cryptocurrencies Rates Commodities Currencies Singapore markets open in 8h 13m Straits Times Index 5,660.52 +25.11 +0.45% Nikkei 64,136.25 +213.25 +0.33% Hang Seng 24,604.29 -109.49 -0.44% FTSE 100 10,816.14 +127.67 +1.19% Bitcoin USD 76,610.07 +908.51 +1.20% FTSE Bursa Malaysia 1,674.74 -4.47 -0.27% ADVERTISEMENT Why Could Bloom Energy (BE) Change AI Data Center Power Economics? Bailey Pemberton Thu, 17 September 2026 at 6:13 am GMT-5 2 min read BE Bloom Energy (NYSE:BE) released a special report on a new 800V DC-native fuel cell power solution tailored for AI data centers.\nThe company says the architecture removes the need for traditional transformers, targeting lower capital spending and reduced power conversion losses.\nBloom Energy describes the 800V system as designed to cut deployment timelines for AI computing sites by simplifying on-site power integration.\nThe 800V DC-native fuel cell design for AI data centers is important context to weigh against the rest of our findings. Our analysis turns up 3 warning signs (1 major) for Bloom Energy as well.\nAI power demand is reshaping how data centers get built, so it can help to compare Bloom's approach with peers across the space 60 AI infrastructure stocks .\nBloom Energy designs and installs solid oxide fuel cell systems for on-site power generation. This 800V DC-native architecture directly targets the growing segment of the electrical equipment sector that focuses on supplying high-density, always-on power for AI data centers.\nSee which insiders are buying and selling Bloom Energy following this latest news.\nThe report puts hard numbers around the pitch. For a 1 GW AI site, Bloom Energy claims a 27% cut in non compute capital spending, equal to US$3.6b, and a 9% reduction in five year total cost of ownership, or US$5.5b. That directly supports the idea that AI power projects could become a much larger slice of the business over time.\nRecent call option interest from Situational Awareness and the focus on AI themed contracts suggest traders are using Bloom Energy as a levered way to express views on data center power. At the same time, Simply Wall St flags a highly volatile share price over the past three months and very large recent earnings growth, so sentiment looks fast moving rather than settled.\nThe clearest check will be whether large AI data centers actually adopt 800V DC native architectures at multi hundred megawatt scale. Investors can track Bloom Energy's disclosures around signed 800V projects, contracted megawatts tied to this design, and any timeline updates on AI focused deployments through 2027.\nEverything here focuses on the technology and the projects, but the bigger question is who is steering Bloom Energy and what they are actually rewarded for achieving. See who is actually steering Bloom Energy, and how they are paid .\nThis article by Simply Wall St is general in nature. We provide commentary