      "summary_raw": "Tektronix unveils regenerative test system for 800V AI data center power eeNews Europe",
      "source_text": "Tektronix unveils regenerative test system for 800V AI data center power New Products | September 16, 2026 By Asma Adhimi Power Electronics Tektronix AI data centers Tektronix has introduced the EA-ELR 21000 Dynamic Test System, a regenerative electronic load platform designed to validate the higher-voltage DC power architectures now being developed for AI data centers. The system supports testing of ±400 V and 800 V DC designs while returning absorbed test energy to the grid at up to 95% regenerative efficiency. For eeNews Europe readers working in power electronics, data center infrastructure and test, the launch highlights how quickly AI is pushing power distribution toward higher voltages and much higher rack-level power. It also shows how test equipment is evolving to reproduce the fast-changing loads created by AI computing systems. Regenerative testing up to 1.92 MW The EA-ELR 21000 is built around Tektronix’s new EA-ELR 21000-80 4U HS regenerative electronic load module, which is rated for up to 1,000 V, 80 A and 30 kW. Modules can be combined in 30 kW increments to create systems with up to 1.92 MW of test capacity across eight racks and 64 loads. A single rack can provide up to 240 kW in a footprint of around 0.6 square meters. The system provides current slew rates of up to 12 A/µs at 240 kW, allowing engineers to reproduce the rapid load changes associated with AI workloads. This is intended to help identify voltage droop, recovery and stability problems in power shelves and racks before deployment. Unlike conventional dissipative electronic loads, the system sends absorbed energy back to the grid rather than converting it primarily into heat. Tektronix says regenerative efficiency can reach 95%, reducing cooling requirements and facility power demand during long-duration burn-in, endurance and lifetime testing. “AI is making power architecture a strategic design decision,” said James Hitchcock, Vice President and General Manager of the EA Portfolio at Tektronix. “The systems built for earlier generations of compute were not designed for the scale or pace of today’s AI workloads, and power systems can no longer be treated as one-size-fits-all. The opportunity is to design and validate more efficient, resilient power architectures for the AI infrastructure being built today, with test systems that can keep pace with the speed and scale of the change.” Targeting 800 V data center architectures AI data centers are increasingly exploring ±400 V sidecar architectures and 800 V DC distribution as operators look to reduce current, conversion losses and the quantity of copper needed for power delivery. Tektronix is positioning the EA-ELR 21000 for validation of 800 V power shelves, power racks and solid-state transformers. Integrated waveform generation supports repeatable load profiles, while synchronized controls allow multiple electronic loads to operate as a coordinated system. Later this fall, Tektronix also plans to introduce the EA-ELR 20080-1000 4U HS, an 80 V, 1,000 A regenerative load aimed at testing the 48 V to 54 V power rail closer to AI processors. The EA-ELR 21000 system and 30 kW EA-ELR 21000-80 4U HS module are available now. If you enjoyed this article, you will like the following ones: don't miss them by subscribing to : eeNews on Google News Share:\nTektronix unveils regenerative test system for 800V AI data center power September 16, 2026 Optical MEMS microphone pushes dynamic range to 136 dB August 31, 2026 ROHM pushes 650 V IGBT losses lower for EV auxiliary systems August 31, 2026 BrainBoard1500 brings neuromorphic AI to embedded developers August 24, 2026 IBASE launches edge AI computer with up to 99 TOPS July 26, 2026\nEurope Analog Automotive Embedded Power Wireless Smart2.0 ECINews.fr About About eeNews Europe Power Management Contact Learning center White Papers Webinars News Business Market Technology New products Interviews Featured Articles Events Other sites eeNews Eu