      "summary_raw": "Report from 800 Former EPA Staff Links AI Data Center Power Demand to Asthma and Early Deaths Medical Daily",
      "source_text": "Vitality Report from 800 Former EPA Staff Links AI Data Center Power Demand to Asthma and Early Deaths Published Sep 14, 2026 3:01 PM EDT By Joseph James Anna Moneymaker | Getty Images Share on Twitter Share on Facebook Share on Pocket A group of more than 800 former Environmental Protection Agency employees says the rapid buildout of artificial intelligence data centers could measurably worsen air pollution and public health at the same time federal regulators are loosening the rules meant to limit it. The warning came in a report released on September 10 by the Environmental Protection Network , a nonpartisan organization of former EPA scientists, toxicologists, engineers and policy analysts, many of whom spent decades inside the agency. Who is speaking matters here. These are former career regulators, including people who wrote and enforced the pollution rules now under review. The report identifies at least 30 federal actions taken since January 2025 that the group argues raise pollution-related health risk, weaken safeguards or make enforcement harder. Seventeen of the 30 explicitly cite or target AI or data centers. For most households, the exposure pathway is not the building down the road. It is the power plant that runs it, often many miles away. That is why a family with an asthmatic child can be affected by a facility they will never see. Where the Pollution Actually Comes From Data centers themselves emit relatively little. Their health footprint comes from the electricity they consume and the equipment backing them up: gas-fired and coal-fired plants meeting surging demand, on-site gas turbines, and banks of diesel generators that run during outages and routine testing. Those sources release fine particulate matter and nitrogen oxides, pollutants linked to asthma attacks, heart and lung disease, and premature death. The split between local and regional sources is the report's most useful technical point. In the modeling the report relies on, more than 90 percent of the estimated health impact came from power plants supplying electricity to data centers rather than from emissions at the sites themselves. That makes this a regional air quality question as much as a local siting fight. The report also notes that rising demand is reshaping the power system. Plans to retire or convert nearly 10 gigawatts of coal capacity were delayed as load grew, and an Environmental Integrity Project analysis counted at least 74 proposed gas plants dedicated to data centers. Modeled Projections, Not Counted Cases The health figures in the report come from a 2026 academic analysis by researchers at the University of California, Riverside, the California Institute of Technology and the Rochester Institute of Technology, who used EPA air quality modeling tools. That work estimates that data center-related air pollution could impose roughly $11.7 billion to $20.9 billion in annual public health costs by 2028. Under the study's high-growth scenario, the researchers estimate roughly 600,000 asthma symptom cases and 1,300 premature deaths a year. Those numbers deserve a clear label. They are modeled projections built on growth and emissions assumptions, not observed cases or death certificates, which is one reason the cost estimate spans a range rather than landing on a single figure. The date attached to them also matters, because the estimates describe a future year rather than current conditions. The former officials argue the projections may understate the problem, because the modeling does not account for the 30 federal actions the report documents. The report concedes the gap runs the other way too: EPN says it found no public analysis quantifying how those actions will change the burden. Larry Starfield, former principal deputy assistant administrator in EPA's enforcement office, put the enforcement point plainly, saying that \"passing a law is not effective unless you enforce it.\" The buildout driving the estimates is well documented. Pew Research Center, drawing on Data Center Map, counted more than 3,000 operating data centers in the United States and more than 1,500 more in various stages of development. Pew also found that 38 percent of Americans live within five miles of at least one operating facility. The Burden Falls Unevenly National averages hide what matters most to readers. The underlying academic work found that the per-household health burden in the most affected communities could be about 200 times that of less affected ones, and that economically disadvantaged areas absorb a disproportionate share, partly because of their proximity to power plants and generator banks. The UC Riverside authors also found the pollution crosses county and state lines, so communities that receive no tax revenue from a facility can still carry its health costs. Separate Virginia Commonwealth University research mapped emissions from 138 Northern Virginia data centers across Arlington, Fairfax, Loudoun and Prince William",
