      "summary_raw": "Enphase's bidirectional GaN solid-state transformer enters production in the US, targeting 5MW power delivery for AI data centers finance.biggo.com",
      "source_text": "Enphase's bidirectional GaN solid-state transformer enters production in the US, targeting 5MW power delivery for AI data centers Add preferred source 1 Enphase Energy has announced that its solid-state transformer (SST) power modules utilizing bidirectional gallium nitride (GaN) technology have officially entered production in the United States, marking a key step toward commercial-scale manufacturing for the next-generation power conversion architecture. The modules will be used to assemble full-size IQ® SST racks. Each rack integrates 342 4kW modules in series-parallel configuration to deliver 1.25MW, and multiple racks can be combined to provide up to 5MW of power output for AI data centers, directly supplying 800 VDC to AI computing cabinets. A full system demonstration is scheduled for November 2026, with customer pilot programs beginning in 2027. Compared with traditional back-to-back MOSFET architectures, the bidirectional GaN approach reduces chip footprint to one-quarter and lowers costs by approximately 4x, achieving 98.5% system efficiency and targeting 99.999% availability. Since June this year, bidirectional GaN has been adopted across multiple applications including IAV's main drive inverter, United Automotive Electronic Systems' onboard charging unit, and Enphase's IQ9S-3P microinverter. Suppliers including Innoscience, Infineon, China Resources Microelectronics, and Zhineng have also completed device-level deployments, accelerating the commercialization of bidirectional GaN technology.\nEnphase Energy (ENPH), the leading US microinverter maker, has announced that its solid-state transformer (SST) power modules utilizing bidirectional gallium nitride (GaN) technology have officially begun production in the United States, marking the technology's formal entry into commercial-scale manufacturing. According to the company's official announcement released on September 8, these power modules will be used to assemble full-size IQ® SST racks, with a complete system demonstration planned for November 2026 and customer pilot programs scheduled to begin in 2027. Delivering 5MW-Class Power for AI Data Centers The core design philosophy of the IQ SST is to integrate hundreds of 4kW power modules within a single rack working in concert, ultimately delivering up to 5MW of power output for AI data centers. This approach directly addresses the urgent demand for high power density and high efficiency in AI computing infrastructure. In its Bidirectional GaN Technology White Paper , Enphase states bluntly that existing power conversion architectures — whether based on interleaved parallel topologies, multi-stage designs, or even advanced unidirectional GaN solutions — struggle to simultaneously balance complexity, power density, and cost in inverter design. Bidirectional GaN technology inherits all the performance and cost advantages of its predecessors, including higher efficiency, high-frequency switching capability, reliability, and the cost benefits derived from the BDS (bidirectional switch) structure. The white paper discloses striking figures: the single-stage architecture based on GaN BDS simplifies the microinverter system, and compared with traditional back-to-back MOSFET solutions, bidirectional GaN reduces chip footprint to one-quarter and lowers costs by approximately 4x. According to earlier industry media reports, the IQ SST also eliminates centralized power conversion architecture, using 342 power modules to achieve 1.25MW o",
