      "summary_raw": "Nexperia and Aurobay to explore GaN and SiC power devices for electrified powertrains Charged EVs",
      "source_text": "Home EV Engineering EV Fleets & Infrastructure Off-Highway Electrification Magazine Subscribe Past Issues Change your address Cancel your subscription Conferences EV Engineering Conference EV Infrastructure Newswire Features Engineering Features Vehicle Features Infrastructure Features Contact Advertise Webinars Sponsored Content Industry Events Privacy Policy Terms of Use Search for: EV Fleet & Infrastructure EV Engineering News Nexperia and Aurobay to explore GaN and SiC power devices for electrified powertrains Posted September 11, 2026 by Chris Ruoff & filed under Newswire , The Tech . Nexperia and Aurobay Technologies will explore a collaboration on gallium nitride (GaN) and silicon carbide (SiC) power semiconductors for electrified and hybrid vehicle platforms. Aurobay Technologies is a division of Horse Powertrain , a joint venture between Renault Group and Geely, and the collaboration aims to combine Nexperia’s GaN and SiC work with Aurobay’s powertrain system design and integration capabilities. The collaboration follows an earlier project in which Nexperia’s GaN devices were used in Aurobay’s integrated power generation system for range extenders. Nexperia says that project showed the potential for wide-bandgap devices to improve efficiency and reduce system size and weight in demanding automotive environments. From there, the companies plan to look at power electronics architectures for applications such as traction inverters and power generation systems. They also intend to evaluate joint engineering approaches, including early-stage co-design and system-level optimization. GaN and SiC devices switch faster than silicon IGBTs and dissipate less energy doing it, so an inverter of a given rating can use smaller passive components and less cooling hardware. Hybrid and range-extender drivetrains are packaging-constrained, because the power electronics share space with an engine and its generator. “By working closely with Aurobay Technologies, we can better understand system-level requirements and evaluate how advanced semiconductor technologies can deliver measurable benefits at the vehicle level,” said Edoardo Merli, Nexperia’s Head of Business Group Wide-Bandgap, IGBTs and Modules. “Our experience integrating Nexperia’s GaN technology into a highly integrated power generation system demonstrated the potential of close collaboration across the value chain,” said Ma Yongquan, Aurobay Technologies’ Chief Engineer, Advanced Engineering on Electrical Components. Source: Nexperia Share on Facebook Share Share on Twitter Tweet Share on Pinterest Share Share on LinkedIn Share Send email Mail\nAdvancing Battery Safety Through Early Thermal Runaway Detection With Infineon Sensors Register\nScalable And Cost-Efficient Testing Of State-of-the-Art Battery Management Systems Register\nCoolGaN™ Automotive Bidirectional Switch: Shaping Single-Stage On-Board Chargers Register\nMicroseconds Matter: High-Fidelity FPGA Motor Models For Real-Time HIL Validation Register\nSimplifying Commercial EV Power Distribution With Integrated Off-the-Shelf Solutions Register\nFrom Lab To Vehicle: Driving Reliable Insulation Systems For E-mobility Innovation Register\nDriving Efficiency In EV Manufacturing: Ultrasonic Solutions For Critical Connections Register\nHow To Test For Anti-Islanding In EV Chargers, OBCs, And V2G Systems Register\nMultiphysics Modeling Of Transport Phenomena In Cells With Gas Diffusion Electrodes Register\nOptimizing HV/LV Power Conversion For Next-Generation xEV Architectures Register\nBeyond Sealing: Extrusion And Co-Extrusion For Thermal, Electrical, And Sensing Applications Register\nGet Daily EV Industry News In Your Inbox: I agree to your Privacy Policy and Terms of Service. Subscribe\nEV Engineering Webinars & Whitepapers A comprehensive PSA-tape-based strategy for thermal runaway prevention and mitigation in battery packs Revolutionizing commercial EVs: megawatt charging and its implications Res