      "source_text": "Energy World’s first 22.9kV solid-state transformer developed to electrify US data centers Hyosung’s high-voltage solid-state transformer uses power semiconductors to control electricity. Read Next: New HALEU deal brings compact nuclear reactors closer to military bases, space By Aamir Khollam Energy Sep 07, 2026 07:08 PM EST By Aamir Khollam Sep 07, 2026 07:08 PM EST 22.9kV solid-state transformer (SST) Hyosung Heavy Industries The rapid expansion of US data centers is creating a power problem that conventional grid equipment may struggle to solve. Hyosung Heavy Industries says it has developed a 22.9kV-class solid-state transformer (SST) aimed at the next generation of power networks. The South Korean power equipment maker developed a 1.05MVA SST in 2022. It says the system can connect directly to urban distribution networks. The technology could become increasingly relevant as American utilities face rising loads from data centers and aging infrastructure. Solid-state transformers enter Unlike conventional transformers, SSTs use power semiconductors to actively control electricity. That gives operators tighter control over voltage and current across the grid. The technology also retains electrical isolation functions found in traditional transformers. Its digital control capabilities could make SSTs useful for future distribution systems with more complex power demands. Hyosung describes the 22.9kV market as an emerging segment. Large data centers require substantial amounts of electricity, creating demand for equipment capable of handling higher distribution voltages. Global SST demand could grow by more than 40% annually, according to market research cited by Hyosung. However, high-voltage systems remain difficult to commercialize. Power semiconductors alone do not solve that challenge. Engineers must also manage thermal loads, insulation, switching behavior, and complex power-control requirements. More from Energy See All Energy Californi