      "summary_raw": "CEA Proposes 15 Percent Grid-Forming Inverters, 10 Percent ESS for New RE Projects from July 2027 Energetica India Magazine",
      "source_text": "Energy Guide Guide online Free signup Advertise my Company Power Plants Overview Add your plant --> Magazine Free subscription magazine Last edition July-August 2026 All magazines Our bloggers Bharat Vasandani Madhavan N. & Hari... Urvish Dave Home › Policies & Regulations ›CEA Proposes 15 Percent Grid-Forming Inverters, 10 Percent ESS for New RE Projects from July 2027 CEA Proposes 15 Percent Grid-Forming Inverters, 10 Percent ESS for New RE Projects from July 2027 The CEA has proposed requiring at least 15 percent of inverters in renewable energy plants and all BESS PCS to have grid-forming control for projects commissioned from July 1, 2027. It also proposed 10 percent co-located ESS with two-hour storage for ground-mounted solar and onshore wind projects from July 2027, rising to four hours from July 2029. September 03, 2026. By Mrinmoy Dey The Central Electricity Authority has proposed requiring at least 15 percent grid-forming inverters and 10 percent co-located ESS with two-hour storage for ground-mounted solar and renewable projects commissioned from July 1, 2027, with the ESS duration increasing to four hours from July 1, 2029. The Central Electricity Authority (CEA) has proposed amendments to Technical Standards for Construction of Electric Plants and Electric Lines that would require renewable energy power plants commissioned from July 1, 2027, to have at least 15 percent of inverters with grid-forming control. In addition, all power conversion systems (PCS) of battery energy storage systems (BESS) would need to have grid-forming control to comply with the technical requirements specified under the CEA's grid connectivity regulations. For ground-mounted solar and onshore wind projects commissioned after July 1, 2027, the draft proposes mandatory co-located energy storage systems (ESS) with a minimum storage duration of two hours and capacity equivalent to at least 10 percent of the installed plant capacity. For example, a 100 MW solar plant would require at least 10 MW of ESS with two hours of storage. The proposed requirements would become more stringent for projects commissioned after July 1, 2029, and up to June 30, 2031. Such ground-mounted solar and onshore wind plants would need co-located ESS with a minimum storage duration of four hours, while the storage capacity would remain at 10 percent of the plant's installed capacity. Thus, a 100 MW project would require at least 10 MW of four-hour storage. The draft also provides for flexibility in the future, stating that the CEA may notify changes to the percentage requirement for grid-forming capability or ESS capacity from time to time. Stakeholders can submit suggestions, comments and objections to CEA by October 4, 2026. Download the attached file Tags: Policies & Regulations Central Electricity Authority CEA draft amendment Grid forming inverters Grid forming capability in BESS Grid forming inverts in RE projects ESS in solar and onshore wind projects Please share! --> --> --> --> --> --> If you want to cooperate with us and would like to reuse some of our content, please contact: contact@energetica-india.net . Next events ACHEMA 2027 June 14, 2027 ... more information Inter Solar Europe 2027 June 08, 2027 ... more information SNEC SMART E 20th (2027) International Photovoltaic Power Generation and Smart Energy Conference and Exhibition June 02, 2027 ... more information RenewX India 2027 April 22, 2027 ... more information The smarter E India – India’s Innovation Hub for the New Energy World March 09, 2027 ... more information All events Last interviews Debasis Goswami Head – Shell E4, Investments, Startup Acceleration & Deployments Deep-Tech Startups Must Bridge Commercialisation Gap, Says Shell India’s Debasis Goswami Dhananjay Kumar Director - Regulatory and Corporate Affairs Renewable Procurement Must Become More Flexible, Scalable, Says Dhananjay Kumar, ENGIE Pritimukta Sarangi Group Director of Sustainability and Technology Renewables, Storage and Granular Energy Matching Key to Sustainable AI Growth: PDG Pratik Kamdar Co-founder & CEO Neuron Energy’s Pratik Kamdar Says Scale, R&D and Technology Will Drive Next Phase of Growth Naveen Gupta Founder and CEO Naveen Gupta, Trev Mobility, Says Premium EV Mobility Must Compete on Reliability and Service All interviews Follow us\nThe CEA has proposed requiring at least 15 percent of inverters in renewable energy plants and all BESS PCS to have grid-forming control for projects commissioned from July 1, 2027. It also proposed 10 percent co-located ESS with two-hour storage for ground-mounted solar and onshore wind projects from July 2027, rising to four hours from July 2029.\nDeep-Tech Startups Must Bridge Commercialisation Gap, Says Shell India’s Debasis Goswami\nRenewable Procurement Must Become More Flexible, Scalable, Says Dhananjay Kumar, ENGIE\nRenewables, Storage and Granular Energy Matching Key to Sustainable AI Growth: PDG\nNeuron Energy’s Pratik Kamdar Says Scale, R&D and Technology Wil",
