      "source_text": "Eaton Showcases 800V Solid-State Transformer at SEMICON; Cumulative Investment in Taiwan Exceeds NT$200 Million Add preferred source Eaton showcased a comprehensive power supply portfolio designed for AI data centers and the semiconductor industry at SEMICON Taiwan 2026, debuting the 9395XR high-power UPS, the 93PR compact UPS, and an 800V medium-voltage solid-state transformer (MV SST) in Taiwan for the first time. The company also jointly exhibited liquid cooling solutions with Boyd Thermal. Lin Yu-pei, general manager of Eaton Electrical Taiwan and the Philippines, said cumulative investment in Taiwan exceeded NT$200 million (approximately $6.3 million) in 2025–2026, with plans to invest an additional NT$300 million (approximately $9.5 million) over the next four years. He projected that AI data center power architectures will evolve from 48V toward 800VDC, with future AI rack power demand potentially exceeding 300kW. At the same event, Omron showcased CT-type 3D X-ray inspection solutions and a collaboration with NVIDIA Omniverse, while Advantech focused on Edge AI and Physical AI technologies—signaling that the semiconductor supply chain is moving toward integrated solutions.\nPower management giant Eaton showcased a complete power supply portfolio designed for AI data centers and the semiconductor industry at SEMICON Taiwan 2026, debuting its 800V medium-voltage solid-state transformer (MV SST) and 9395XR high-power uninterruptible power supply (UPS) in Taiwan for the first time. Lin Yu-pei, general manager of Eaton Electrical Taiwan and the Philippines, said cumulative investment in Taiwan exceeded NT$200 million (approximately $6.3 million) in 2025–2026, with plans to invest an additional NT$300 million (approximately $9.5 million) over the next four years to strengthen local production capacity and service capabilities. Eaton's exhibition was centered on the theme \"From Grid to Chip,\" covering four major areas: high-efficiency UPS systems, DC power supply, data center power distribution and cooling, and circuit protection and electronic components. Lin noted: \"The end of computing power is electricity. On the surface, AI competition is about improving computing efficiency and scale, but the most critical foundation underpinning operations is the stability of power system load capacity.\" He believes that in the operating model of AI factories, electricity has become a production factor that directly affects token output efficiency. Whether every watt of power can be efficiently and stably converted into computing power will directly impact the operational efficiency of factories and data centers. Three New Products Debut One of the biggest highlights of the exhibition was the Taiwan debut of the 9395XR UPS, with maximum power capacity of 2.5MW. Its single-unit, large-capacity design helps reduce the number of devices and footprint required. Nearly 1,000 units have already been deployed across Asia. Another newly launched product, the 93PR compact UPS, achieves up to 600kW capacity per unit with power modules occupying just 3U of rack space, delivering power density of 1MW per square meter. It enables existing facilities to rapidly boost power supply capacity without expansion, addressing the power demands of AI and high-performance computing. On the DC power supply front, the MV SST—exhibited for the first time—accepts 10–34.5kV medium-voltage input directly and converts it to 800VDC, reducing conversion stages compared to traditional power supply architectures and achieving overall power conversion efficiency of up to 98.5%. The product has received TÜV SÜD Mark certification from the global third-party testing organization TÜV SÜD, having passed rigorous IEC 62477-2 testing, and is ready for commercial deployment in global markets. Lin projected that AI data center power architectures will evolve from 48V toward 800VDC, with future AI rack power demand potentially exceeding 300kW. Integrated Liquid Cooling Extends Thermal Management Footprint Eaton also jointly exhibited liquid cooling solutions with Boyd Thermal, a company acquired by the group, for the first time. The solution is compatible with NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 and NVIDIA GB300 NVL72 platforms, delivering precise cooling directly to GPUs, CPUs, and high-speed networking chips through high-efficiency cold plates. Combined with Inner Manifold and Rack Manifold designs, it supports high-density, fully liquid-cooled AI rack architectures that meet the performance and specification requirements of the NVIDIA MGX platform. In power distribution products, the data center-specific XAP-Track Pro busway features a track-based design where plug-in units can be installed at any point along the busway, replacing traditional power distribution cabinets and significantly reducing installation time and labor costs. The rack-mount PDU G4 power distribution unit features C39 outlets that combine C13 and C19 specifications, compatible with both C14 and C20 ",
