      "summary_raw": "Intrinsic polarization superjunctions in III-nitride heterostructures for efficient power electronics nature.com",
      "source_text": "Intrinsic polarization superjunctions in III-nitride heterostructures for efficient power electronics Download PDF Download PDF Article Open access Published: 28 August 2026 Intrinsic polarization superjunctions in III-nitride heterostructures for efficient power electronics Luca Mazzone ORCID: orcid.org/0009-0000-8578-2942 1 na1 , Yuan Zong ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-6036-2711 1 na1 , Hongkeng Zhu ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-8977-0199 1 , Alessandro Mauro ORCID: orcid.org/0009-0003-4851-6655 1 & … Elison Matioli ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-6475-001X 1 Show authors Nature Electronics ( 2026 ) Cite this article Save article View saved research 13k Accesses 79 Altmetric Metrics details Abstract III-nitride semiconductors combine a wide bandgap with low sheet resistance and, thus, can be used for efficient power electronics. Their lateral architecture also enables monolithic integration on cost-effective silicon. However, electric-field crowding, as well as surface and buffer trapping, limit the breakdown voltage, degrade dynamic performance and reduce reliability. Here we report intrinsic polarization superjunctions in III-nitride heterostructures for efficient power electronics. We use the spontaneous and piezoelectric polarization fields to induce matched mobile electrons in a two-dimensional electron gas and mobile holes in a two-dimensional hole gas, forming an intrinsically charge-balanced superjunction without intentional doping. With an ohmic contact to the two-dimensional hole gas, both carrier types can be dynamically depleted during switching, which preserves drift-region charge neutrality, producing a nearly uniform electric field and increasing the breakdown voltage under operating conditions. We use the approach to create gallium-nitride-on-silicon polarization superjunction Schottky barrier diodes with a breakdown voltage exceeding 3.9 kV and a specific ON resistance as low as 4.7 mΩ cm 2 , as well as polarization superjunction transistors with a breakdown voltage exceeding 3.4 kV. The devices exhibit a near-ideal dynamic ON-resistance degradation of less than 15% up to 3 kV, as well as repeatable, temperature-stable and avalanche-like breakdown.\nSimilar content being viewed by others Interface engineering for facile switching of bulk-strong polarization in Si-compatible vertical superlattices Article Open access 21 March 2024\nCumulative polarization in conductive interfacial ferroelectrics Article 09 November 2022\nExperimental determination of giant polarization in wurtzite III-nitride semiconductors Article Open access 24 April 2025\nExplore related subjects Discover the latest articles and news in related subjects. Electrical and electronic engineering Nanoscience and technology\nMain Wide-bandgap and ultrawide-bandgap semiconductors are central to the development of power electronics that can sustain high voltages with greater efficiency, higher power density and lower cost. Their wide bandgaps result in high critical electric fields ( \\({E}_{{\\rm{cr}}}\\approx \\,{E}_{\\,\\text{g}\\,}^{2.5}\\) ; E g is the bandgap) 1 , combined with their ability to be doped effectively as well as preserve good carrier transport properties 2 . The static characteristics of power electronic devices are governed by a trade-off between specific ON resistance ( R ON,sp ) and breakdown voltage ( V BR ) 3 , 4 , and a high E cr can lead to shorter drift regions for a given V BR , reducing R ON,sp and allowing devices to handle high power with low conduction losses. III-nitride power devices are typically created with lateral architectures based on barrier/gallium nitride (GaN) heterostructures, where the barrier is formed by nitride compounds containing one or more of the elements aluminium, gallium, indium, and scandium (Al,Ga,In,Sc)-N. The spontaneous and piezoelectric polarization discontinuity at the barrier/GaN interface induces a two-dimensional electron gas (2DEG) 5 . This high-carrier-density high-mobility channel provides high conductivity, enhancing device efficiency and enabling high-frequency operation, which reduces the size of passive components in power converters. The lateral nature of GaN devices also supports the monolithic integration of power switches with gate drivers and logic circuits. In addition, GaN can be epitaxially grown on low-cost, large-diameter silicon (Si) substrates (eight inches and beyond) 6 , unlike other wide-bandgap and ultrawide-bandgap semiconductors that are restricted to smaller and more expensive wafers. The development of lateral GaN devices does, however, face a fundamental challenge: the non-uniform OFF-state electric-field distribution in the depletion region between the gate (or anode) and drain (or cathode). The electric field peaks sharply at the contact edges, causing premature breakdown and under-utilization of the drift region, which restricts the effective V BR relative to the intrinsic potential of GaN 7 , 8 , 9 , 10 , 11 . The resulting electric-field crowding also r",
